# Computer Vision Coding Rules (1–51)

Project: Python Computer Vision
Stack: Python, uv, FastAPI, Uvicorn, OpenCV (`import cv2 as cv`)
Architecture: `main.py` → `routes` → `controllers` → `config`
Principle: Child-readable code, adult-level production safety.

Write the simplest code a beginner can understand, while keeping the security, correctness, and reliability expected from a professional engineer.

## Project shape

```
python-CV/
├── main.py
├── config/
│   └── cv_config.py
├── controllers/
│   └── cv_controller.py
├── routes/
│   └── cv_routes.py
├── pyproject.toml
└── uv.lock
```

Flow: camera or image → OpenCV import → configuration → controller → route → FastAPI response.

`main.py` creates the FastAPI app and registers routes. `routes/cv_routes.py` defines HTTP endpoints. `controllers/cv_controller.py` implements vision operations. `config/cv_config.py` holds `import cv2 as cv` and shared settings.

## Rules

01. SIMPLICITY FIRST. Write code a beginner can read. Choose the simplest solution that is correct, safe, and maintainable.
02. PRODUCTION QUALITY. This is a learning project. Still write reliable, secure, professional-quality code.
03. DESCRIPTIVE NAMING. Names for variables, functions, classes, routes, and files must explain their purpose.
04. NO UNNECESSARY COMPLEXITY. Skip advanced patterns, clever tricks, and extra architecture unless they solve a real problem.
05. ONE CLEAR RESPONSIBILITY. Each function and file has one job. Split code only when that improves understanding or maintenance.
06. MINIMUM FILES. Create the fewest files and folders necessary. Do not add files only because larger frameworks use them.
07. INLINE CODE WHEN APPROPRIATE. Prefer readable in-house functions over extra helper files, wrappers, and abstractions.
08. PRESERVE PROJECT ARCHITECTURE. Follow `main.py`, `routes`, `controllers`, and `config`. Do not restructure without a clear reason.
09. SMALLEST SAFE CHANGE. Change only what the current lesson or feature requires. No unrelated refactoring.
10. PRESERVE WORKING CODE. Do not delete, rename, or rewrite working behavior unless the change is necessary and explained.
11. COMPLETE COPY-PASTE CODE. When changing a file, provide its full updated contents, exact path, and any commands needed. No unexplained placeholders.
12. ONE LESSON AT A TIME. Teach and implement one small step. Confirm the current step works before the next.
13. EXPLAIN BEFORE IMPLEMENTING. Say what is being built, why it is needed, where it belongs, and how it connects.
14. WHAT / WHY / HOW / IMPORTANT. Document a file when useful: what it does, why it exists, how it connects, and any critical safety or limitation.
15. MEANINGFUL COMMENTS. Comment important decisions and non-obvious logic. Do not restate simple Python.
16. CONSISTENT CODING STYLE. Readable indentation and consistent formatting. Use camelCase for our own Python functions and variables. Follow each library's own naming.
17. REVIEW BEFORE EDITING. Read the relevant files and imports before changing them. Do not assume how an unseen file is written.
18. MINIMAL DEPENDENCIES. Install only packages that are required. Prefer the standard library and dependencies already in the project.
19. UV PACKAGE MANAGEMENT. Use `uv` for the environment and dependencies. Prefer `uv add`, `uv run`, and the uv project workflow.
20. SIMPLE APPLICATION ENTRY POINT. Keep `main.py` small. It creates the FastAPI app, registers routes, and configures only essential startup behavior.
21. CENTRALIZED CONFIGURATION. Keep the shared OpenCV import and genuinely shared settings in `config/cv_config.py` using `import cv2 as cv`.
22. ROUTES HANDLE HTTP. Routes define endpoints, request parameters, and response boundaries. Do not put substantial image-processing algorithms in route functions.
23. CONTROLLERS HANDLE LOGIC. Controllers implement loading, grayscale, resizing, detection, and other image processing.
24. SERVICES ONLY WHEN NECESSARY. Add services, models, middleware, or extra layers only when a demonstrated requirement justifies them.
25. REUSE BEFORE REWRITING. Reuse existing functions and configuration. Do not duplicate processing or validation.
26. BACKEND IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. Validate requests and control processing on the backend. Do not trust a value only because a frontend sent it.
27. VALIDATE ALL INPUTS. Validate parameters, filenames, image formats, file sizes, dimensions, camera identifiers, and other external input before processing.
28. SAFE IMAGE LOADING. Confirm an image loaded before calling OpenCV. Handle unreadable, empty, and corrupted images.
29. SAFE FILE AND PATH HANDLING. Use controlled directories and safe filenames. Prevent path traversal, unsafe overwrites, and unauthorized file access.
30. UNDERSTAND IMAGE DATA. Know width, height, channels, dtype, pixel values, and array shape before transforming or analyzing an image.
31. EXPLICIT COLOR FORMATS. OpenCV commonly uses BGR. Many other systems use RGB. Convert explicitly when required.
32. EXPLICIT IMAGE TRANSFORMATIONS. Make grayscale, resize, crop, normalize, rotate, and color conversion clear and traceable.
33. PRESERVE IMAGE INTEGRITY. Avoid unnecessary quality loss. Use copies when required. Keep original data distinct from processed data.
34. CONFIGURE IMPORTANT VALUES. Keep camera indexes, image limits, frame dimensions, and model paths in configuration instead of scattering hardcoded values.
35. MANAGE CAMERA RESOURCES. Open a camera only when needed, verify it initialized, and always release the handle.
36. HANDLE ERRORS CLEARLY. Use appropriate exception handling. Return understandable errors. Never hide a failure or a programming bug.
37. HANDLE DISCONNECTIONS. Missing cameras, interrupted streams, unreadable frames, disconnected clients, and missing files must not crash the whole application.
38. PROTECT FASTAPI RESPONSIVENESS. Do not block the async event loop with heavy camera or image work. Use a suitable synchronous or background path when required.
39. PERFORMANCE WITHOUT PREMATURE OPTIMIZATION. Correctness and readability come first. Measure speed, memory, and frame rate before optimizing.
40. CONTROL RESOURCE CONSUMPTION. Limit image sizes, uploads, buffers, workloads, memory, and concurrent operations.
41. REAL-TIME STREAMING SAFETY. For video, manage frame rate, queues, timestamps, and dropped frames. Do not let an unbounded buffer consume memory.
42. PRIVACY BY DESIGN. Treat camera footage, recordings, and audio as sensitive. Capture, keep, or share only what is needed and authorized.
43. EXPLICIT STORAGE BEHAVIOR. Distinguish temporary frames, saved images, recorded videos, and uploads. Do not silently store user media.
44. AUTHENTICATION AND AUTHORIZATION. Protect camera controls, uploads, recordings, and private endpoints. Enforce access on the backend.
45. SECURE DEPENDENCIES AND SECRETS. Use maintained libraries, avoid extra dependencies, never commit secrets, and do not expose internal debug details to clients.
46. USEFUL LOGGING AND OBSERVABILITY. Log meaningful events, durations, errors, and resource failures. Do not log sensitive video, personal information, or secrets.
47. TEST SUCCESS AND FAILURE PATHS. Cover normal input, missing files, corrupted images, invalid parameters, camera failures, repeated requests, and resource cleanup.
48. MODULAR AI AND MODEL INTEGRATION. Keep detection, classification, tracking, OCR, and future model integrations replaceable. Do not couple the app to one model or provider.
49. SAFE AND HONEST AI OUTPUTS. Distinguish predictions from verified facts. Handle uncertainty, confidence, bad detections, and failed inference without inventing observations.
50. TRACE THE COMPLETE DATA FLOW. Be able to explain camera or image → OpenCV import → configuration → controller → route → FastAPI response. Know where data enters, how it changes, and what the user receives.
51. NO EXTRA FILES. Do not create unnecessary files, folders, wrappers, helpers, services, or dependencies. Add structure only when it materially improves readability, correctness, security, or maintainability.

## Example

```python
# config/cv_config.py
import cv2 as cv

# controllers/cv_controller.py
from config.cv_config import cv

def toGrayscale(image):
    if image is None:
        raise ValueError("Image failed to load")
    return cv.cvtColor(image, cv.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)
```
